# CRM gate — this project's seam for rasa.module.crm

This file is **project-owned**. `rasa.module.crm` copied it once at install
(`skip-if-exists`) and will never overwrite it. It holds the judgement the
module cannot make for a business it has never seen:

1. **What "qualified" means** — in words, criterion by criterion.
2. **What each stage means** — what has to be true to enter it and to leave it.
3. **What "active customer" and "churn" mean** for your accounts.
4. **What you may record about a person** — the privacy rails for the
   address book.

The machine half of the seam lives in `crm/crm.config.yml`, because the
tools check it: the stage list, the qualification criteria by name, the
lead sources, the account statuses, the contact roles, the groups and the
labels. **`crm lead advance` into the qualified stage hard-stops until
`qualification` is declared there** — and each criterion must be asserted
with `--met` when a lead is qualified. Guessing what "qualified" means for
your business is the one inference this module refuses to make.

Keep the two halves in step: every criterion named in the config gets a
paragraph here, and every stage in the config gets its entry and exit.

Honest defaults are shown below. Replace them — running on the defaults is
a starting point, not a destination.

---

## 1. Qualification — what the criteria mean

The config names the criteria; this section says what each one means, and
what counts as evidence. When a lead is qualified, the person running
`crm lead advance … --met` is saying every one of these is true.

Default (matches `qualification: [need, budget, decision-maker]`):

- **need** — the customer has named, in their own words, a problem we
  solve. Our guess at their problem does not count.
- **budget** — money for this exists in their plan or can be found; the
  number itself can come later.
- **decision-maker** — we know who signs, and have spoken with them or with
  someone who speaks for them.

> _Replace with your own. A self-serve business might use
> `[activated-trial, fits-profile]`; a services firm
> `[scoped, budget-range-agreed, sponsor-named]`._

## 2. Stages — entry and exit

One line per stage in `stages` (default below). Entry is what must be true
to move a lead in; exit is what moves it on.

- **new** — entry: captured, with the account it is against. Exit: first
  real contact made.
- **contacted** — entry: a conversation has started. Exit: the
  qualification criteria are met (→ qualified), or it goes cold (→ lost).
- **qualified** — entry: every criterion in §1 is true (the tool enforces
  that each is asserted). Exit: a proposal or quote is sent.
- **proposal** — entry: a proposal or quote is out. Exit: they say yes
  (→ won) or no (→ lost).
- **won** — `crm lead win`: the deal is agreed. The account becomes a
  current customer in the same act.
- **lost** — `crm lead lose --reason`: it did not happen. The reason is
  the most useful thing a lost lead keeps; write it for the person who
  reads it in a year.

> _Add, rename or remove stages in the config, then describe them here._

## 3. Accounts — active and churned

What your account statuses mean. The tool moves an account to the active
status when one of its leads is won; every other change is a person's
decision, made with `crm account status`.

- **prospect** — we are selling to them; nothing won yet.
- **active** — a current customer: at least one deal won and the
  relationship is live.
- **churned** — was a customer, is not any more.

**When is an account churned?** Default: when the customer ends the
relationship or stops buying. Say what that means here — a contract not
renewed? no order in N months? an explicit cancellation? — so two people
make the same call.

> _If you declared other statuses (paused, at-risk), define them here._

## 4. The address book — what may be recorded about a person

A contact card is **personal data about someone else**. These rails decide
what goes on it; the tools cannot tell a fact that is fine to keep from one
that is not.

Default rails (tighten them for your setting — a clinic, a school or a law
firm will have stricter rules of its own):

- Record what the relationship needs: how to reach the person, their role
  and organization, and the notes a colleague would need to pick up the
  conversation.
- Do not record sensitive details — health, religion, politics, finances,
  family circumstances — unless the relationship genuinely requires them
  and the person would expect it.
- Birthdays and relationships (spouse, assistant) only when the person
  shared them or they are public.
- Imported address books bring in everything the source had. Review an
  import; remove what does not meet these rails.
- When a person asks to be forgotten, or a legal duty requires it, erasing
  their card is a deliberate act outside the tools (see the family rules,
  "Archive, never delete") — note in your own records that it was done.

> _Name any regulation or policy that applies here, and who decides._

## 5. Roles and sources — optional notes

If you declared `contact_roles` or `sources` in the config, a line each on
what they mean helps everyone file consistently:

> _decision-maker — signs or approves the deal. champion — pushes it
> forward inside the account. referral — a customer or partner sent them._
